## law
law-resistance = Electrical resistance
law-resistance-statement-novice = Resistance shows how much a material “gets in the way” of a current. A metal hardly does, salt water a little, pure water and oil a lot.
law-resistance-statement-school = The resistance of a conductor is R = ρ·L/S: the longer the conductor and the smaller its cross-section, the larger the resistance; ρ is the resistivity of the material.
law-resistance-story-school = This is the definition of a physical quantity; its unit is named after Georg Ohm.
law-ohm = Ohm’s law
law-ohm-statement-novice = The higher the voltage, the larger the current. The higher the resistance, the smaller the current.
law-ohm-statement-school = The current in a section of a circuit is proportional to the voltage across it and inversely proportional to its resistance: I = U/R.
law-ohm-story-school = The German physics teacher Georg Ohm closed a circuit with wires of different length, thickness and material and measured the current. In 1827 he published the results in the book “The galvanic circuit investigated mathematically”. Critics at first met it with hostility — recognition came only in the 1840s.
law-ohm-story-advanced = As early as 1781 Henry Cavendish, experimenting with glass tubes of salt solution, came to a similar conclusion, but his notes were published by Maxwell only in 1879.
law-ohm-limits-school = For a liquid under current Ohm’s law gets a little more complex: part of the voltage goes into splitting the liquid, and heating lowers its resistance.
law-ohm-limits-advanced = Ohm’s law holds for metals and solutions at constant temperature. In a liquid under current a decomposition voltage adds up, and heating changes the resistance.
law-joule-lenz = Joule–Lenz law
law-joule-lenz-statement-novice = A current heats the conductor. The larger the current and the longer it flows, the more heat.
law-joule-lenz-statement-school = The heat a current releases in a conductor equals the square of the current times the resistance times the time: Q = I²·R·t.
law-joule-lenz-story-school = The English physicist James Joule first published this law in December 1840. Independently, in 1842, the Baltic German physicist Heinrich Lenz studied how currents heat conductors — that is why the law is named after both.
law-faraday-electrolysis = Faraday’s law of electrolysis
law-faraday-electrolysis-statement-novice = A current can split liquids. The more charge passes, the more substance forms at the electrodes.
law-faraday-electrolysis-statement-school = The mass of a substance released at an electrode in electrolysis is proportional to the charge passed through the electrolyte: m = k·q.
law-faraday-electrolysis-story-school = Michael Faraday published the laws of electrolysis in 1833–1834. He also introduced the words we still use: electrolysis, electrode, electrolyte, anode, cathode, anion and cation. Splitting water by current into hydrogen and oxygen was first achieved by Nicholson and Carlisle back in 1800.
law-series = Conductors in series
law-series-statement-novice = Conductors one after another: the same current in them, the source’s voltage shared between them.
law-series-statement-school = In series the current is the same in all conductors: I = I₁ = I₂; the total voltage is the sum: U = U₁ + U₂; the total resistance is the sum: R = R₁ + R₂.
law-series-story-school = These rules follow from the laws the German physicist Gustav Kirchhoff stated in 1845: as much current as flows into a junction flows out; and the voltages around a closed loop add up to the source’s.
law-parallel = Conductors in parallel
law-parallel-statement-novice = Conductors side by side, each on its own branch: the same voltage across them, the current splits.
law-parallel-statement-school = In parallel the voltage is the same across all conductors: U = U₁ = U₂; the current in the main line is the sum of the branch currents: I = I₁ + I₂; the total resistance: 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ — less than the smallest of them.
law-electric-power = Electric power
law-electric-power-statement-novice = Power tells how much energy a device takes from the current each second.
law-electric-power-statement-school = A consumer’s power P = U·I — the voltage across it times the current in it, in watts (1 W = 1 V·A). For a resistor, by Ohm’s law, P = I²·R = U²/R.
law-electric-power-story-school = This is the definition of a quantity.
law-electromagnetic-induction = Electromagnetic induction
law-electromagnetic-induction-statement-novice = Move a magnet near a coil — and a current flows in it. Stop — the current is gone. The faster the motion, the larger the current.
law-electromagnetic-induction-statement-school = When the magnetic flux through a closed loop changes, an induced current flows in it (Faraday’s law): the EMF ε = −N·ΔΦ/Δt — the faster the flux changes and the more turns, the larger it is. The induced current flows so that its field opposes the change of the flux (Lenz’s law): so a magnet pushed in and pulled out, or turned the other pole first, drives the current opposite ways.
law-electromagnetic-induction-story-school = On 29 August 1831 Michael Faraday wrote down in his notebook the first experiment on electromagnetic induction. Independently, Joseph Henry noticed the same in 1832, but Faraday published first. In 1834 Emil Lenz stated the rule that gives the direction of the induced current.
law-electromagnetic-induction-limits-school = The EMF depends on how fast the flux changes, and the current on the circuit’s resistance too: I = ε / R. More turns — more EMF, but also more resistance of the wire.
law-electromagnet = The magnetic field of a current. The electromagnet
law-electromagnet-statement-novice = A wire with a current is a magnet: it turns a compass needle. Wind the wire as a coil on an iron nail — and the magnet gets much stronger.
law-electromagnet-statement-school = An electric current makes a magnetic field. A coil’s field is proportional to the current and to the number of turns, and an iron core strengthens it tens and hundreds of times. Reverse the current — and the electromagnet’s poles swap.
law-electromagnet-story-school = In 1820 the Danish scientist Hans Christian Ørsted found that an electric current makes a magnetic field: a compass needle by a wire with a current turns. In 1824 the Englishman William Sturgeon made the first electromagnet — a coil on an iron core. From 1830 the American Joseph Henry wound many layers of insulated wire — thousands of turns — and got very strong electromagnets.
law-electromagnet-limits-school = Proportional — while the iron is far from saturation (its field well under 1.5 T); beyond that more current hardly strengthens the core. How much the core strengthens depends both on the steel and on the shape: a short thick rod less than a long thin one.
